  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Wednesday...\n\nThursday will see showers and thunderstorms across much of the\nregion as an upper level short wave moves through. Southeasterly \nwinds will advect moisture into the region bringing much above \naverage PWATS (1-1.25 in). The timing of the shortwave will start \nprecipitation Thursday morning in the west before moving across \nSoutheast Montana and strengthening throughout the day. Showers \nand thunderstorms are expected to exit the region Thursday night. \nSoundings indicate an environment with plentiful shear (35-50 kts)\nand moisture but somewhat lacking in instability with generally \n<1,000 J/kg of CAPE. Threats with these storms include >1 inch \nhail, >60 mph winds, and heavy rain. Due to this the Storm \nPrediction Center has issued a risk level 1 of 5 for severe storms\nsouth and east of Billings for Thursday. \n\nShower and thunderstorm chances return late Friday with a similar\natmospheric setup, high PWAT values, strong shear, and weak\ninstability. The Storm Prediction Center has issued a risk level 1\nof 5 for severe storms for locations east of Billings. The threats\nwith these storms are expected to be similar to Thursday. \n\nSoutheast Montana will stay in an active pattern through Monday as\na large 500 mb trough continues to impact the region. Showers and\nthunderstorms are expected again Saturday with atmospheric\nprofiles featuring strong low and mid level lapse rates unlocking\nmore CAPE to go along with more southwesterly shear profiles. \nThis will likely lead to Saturdays storms being more widespread in\ncoverage and potentially having a greater hail and wind threat. \nPrecipitation chances decrease (30-60%) Sunday and Monday with the\nbest chances for the western foothills. The potential for severe \nstorms decreases significantly Sunday and Monday. \n\nTemperatures will be below average with highs in the 70s F for\nmost of the next week. Friday will be the warmest day with many\nareas in the low 80s F. Sunday and Monday will be the coolest days\nwith temperatures in the 60s F for the western foothills.\nTorgerson",
